$WW,1$$FG,5,0$GNU Tools Porting Plan for ZealOS$FG$

$FG,2$Overview$FG$
This plan outlines the phases needed to port GNU coreutils and other
GNU tools to ZealOS using our POSIX compatibility layer.

$FG,9$Current State:$FG$
- POSIX layer: file descriptors, pipes, signals, process management
- POSIX shell: command parsing, pipelines, redirections, control flow
- POSIX utilities: 40+ built-in utilities (cat, ls, grep, etc.)
- Virtual memory: per-process page tables, COW fork
- $FG,2$[DONE]$FG$ libc shim: stdio, stdlib, string, unistd, ctype, etc.

$FG,9$Goal:$FG$
Enable compilation and execution of real GNU tools (coreutils, grep,
sed, awk, etc.) on ZealOS with minimal source modifications.

$FG,2$========================================================================
PHASE 1: libc Compatibility Shim (Foundation)
========================================================================$FG$

$FG,5$Priority: CRITICAL - Everything depends on this$FG$

Create a minimal C library that maps standard C functions to our POSIX layer.
This is NOT a full libc - just enough to compile simple programs.

$FG,9$Files to create:$FG$
  /Home/POSIX/libc/
    stdio.h      - FILE*, printf, scanf, fopen, fclose, etc.
    stdlib.h     - malloc, free, exit, atoi, getenv, etc.
    string.h     - strlen, strcpy, strcmp, memcpy, memset, etc.
    unistd.h     - read, write, close, fork, exec, getpid, etc.
    fcntl.h      - open, fcntl, O_* constants
    errno.h      - errno, E* constants
    sys/types.h  - size_t, ssize_t, pid_t, etc.
    sys/stat.h   - stat, fstat, mkdir, S_* constants
    dirent.h     - opendir, readdir, closedir
    signal.h     - signal, kill, sigaction
    time.h       - time, localtime, strftime
    ctype.h      - isalpha, isdigit, toupper, tolower
    limits.h     - PATH_MAX, INT_MAX, etc.
    stdarg.h     - va_list, va_start, va_end
    stddef.h     - NULL, size_t, ptrdiff_t
    stdint.h     - int8_t, uint32_t, etc.
    assert.h     - assert macro

$FG,9$Key functions needed (priority order):$FG$
  1. Memory: malloc, free, realloc, calloc
  2. String: strlen, strcpy, strncpy, strcmp, strncmp, strcat,
             strchr, strrchr, strstr, memcpy, memset, memmove
  3. I/O: printf, fprintf, sprintf, snprintf, puts, fputs, putchar,
          fopen, fclose, fread, fwrite, fgets, fgetc, fputc,
          fflush, fseek, ftell, rewind, feof, ferror
  4. Process: exit, _exit, abort, atexit
  5. Conversion: atoi, atol, strtol, strtoul, strtod

$FG,2$========================================================================
PHASE 2: Build System / Cross-Compilation
========================================================================$FG$

$FG,5$Priority: HIGH - Need to compile C code for ZealOS$FG$

$FG,9$Option A: Native HolyC wrapper$FG$
  - Write HolyC wrappers that call into our libc shim
  - Pros: Works entirely within ZealOS
  - Cons: Can't compile standard C directly

$FG,9$Option B: Cross-compiler (Recommended)$FG$
  - Build a cross GCC/Clang targeting ZealOS
  - Output flat binaries or simple executable format
  - Load and execute from ZealOS
  - Pros: Can compile real GNU source
  - Cons: Requires external toolchain

$FG,9$Option C: Source-to-HolyC translator$FG$
  - Parse C source, output HolyC
  - Handle differences (types, syntax)
  - Pros: Native execution
  - Cons: Complex translation

$FG,9$Recommended approach:$FG$
  Start with Option A for testing libc, then move to Option B.

$FG,9$Files to create:$FG$
  /Home/POSIX/build/
    Makefile         - Build rules for cross-compilation
    zealos.ld        - Linker script for ZealOS executables
    crt0.ZC          - C runtime startup
    syscalls.ZC      - System call interface

$FG,2$========================================================================
PHASE 3: Core Runtime (_start, argc/argv, exit)
========================================================================$FG$

$FG,5$Priority: HIGH - Programs need to start and exit properly$FG$

$FG,9$C Runtime Startup (crt0):$FG$
  1. Set up stack
  2. Parse command line into argc/argv
  3. Initialize libc (stdio buffers, etc.)
  4. Call main(argc, argv, envp)
  5. Call exit() with return value

$FG,9$Files to create:$FG$
  /Home/POSIX/libc/crt0.ZC
    - _start entry point
    - __libc_init() initialization
    - Argument parsing

  /Home/POSIX/libc/exit.ZC
    - exit(), _exit(), _Exit()
    - atexit() handler registry
    - Cleanup (flush stdio, close fds)

$FG,9$Key structures:$FG$
  extern I64 __argc;
  extern U8 **__argv;
  extern U8 **__environ;

$FG,2$========================================================================
PHASE 4: stdio FILE* Streams
========================================================================$FG$

$FG,5$Priority: HIGH - Most programs use printf/scanf$FG$

Implement buffered I/O with FILE* abstraction.

$FG,9$FILE structure:$FG$
  class FILE {
    I64 fd;              // Underlying file descriptor
    U8 *buf;             // I/O buffer
    I64 buf_size;        // Buffer size
    I64 buf_pos;         // Current position in buffer
    I64 buf_len;         // Valid data in buffer (for read)
    U8  mode;            // Read/write/append
    U8  flags;           // EOF, error, etc.
    U8  buf_mode;        // _IOFBF, _IOLBF, _IONBF
  };

$FG,9$Standard streams:$FG$
  FILE *stdin, *stdout, *stderr;

$FG,9$Functions to implement:$FG$
  - fopen, freopen, fclose
  - fread, fwrite
  - fgetc, fputc, getc, putc, getchar, putchar
  - fgets, fputs, gets (deprecated), puts
  - fprintf, printf, sprintf, snprintf, vfprintf, vprintf, vsprintf
  - fscanf, scanf, sscanf
  - fseek, ftell, rewind, fgetpos, fsetpos
  - fflush, setvbuf, setbuf
  - feof, ferror, clearerr
  - fileno, fdopen

$FG,9$Files to create:$FG$
  /Home/POSIX/libc/stdio.ZC     - FILE operations
  /Home/POSIX/libc/printf.ZC    - printf family
  /Home/POSIX/libc/scanf.ZC     - scanf family

$FG,2$========================================================================
PHASE 5: String and Memory Functions
========================================================================$FG$

$FG,5$Priority: HIGH - Used everywhere$FG$

Most of these are simple and can map to existing ZealOS functions.

$FG,9$string.h functions:$FG$
  strlen, strnlen
  strcpy, strncpy, strlcpy
  strcat, strncat, strlcat
  strcmp, strncmp, strcasecmp, strncasecmp
  strchr, strrchr, strstr, strpbrk, strspn, strcspn, strtok, strtok_r
  memcpy, memmove, memset, memcmp, memchr
  strdup, strndup
  strerror

$FG,9$stdlib.h functions:$FG$
  malloc, free, realloc, calloc
  atoi, atol, atoll, atof
  strtol, strtoll, strtoul, strtoull, strtod, strtof
  abs, labs, llabs
  rand, srand
  qsort, bsort
  getenv, setenv, unsetenv, putenv
  exit, _Exit, abort, atexit
  system

$FG,9$ctype.h functions:$FG$
  isalpha, isdigit, isalnum, isspace, isupper, islower
  ispunct, isprint, isgraph, iscntrl, isxdigit
  toupper, tolower

$FG,9$Files to create:$FG$
  /Home/POSIX/libc/string.ZC
  /Home/POSIX/libc/stdlib.ZC
  /Home/POSIX/libc/ctype.ZC
  /Home/POSIX/libc/memory.ZC   - malloc/free implementation

$FG,2$========================================================================
PHASE 6: Process Functions
========================================================================$FG$

$FG,5$Priority: MEDIUM - Needed for complex tools$FG$

$FG,9$exec family:$FG$
  execl, execle, execlp, execv, execve, execvp, execvpe

$FG,9$process control:$FG$
  fork, vfork
  wait, waitpid, wait3, wait4
  system
  popen, pclose

$FG,9$These largely wrap our existing POSIX layer functions.$FG$

$FG,9$Files to create:$FG$
  /Home/POSIX/libc/process.ZC
  /Home/POSIX/libc/popen.ZC

$FG,2$========================================================================
PHASE 7: Test with Simple GNU Tools
========================================================================$FG$

$FG,5$Priority: VALIDATION$FG$

Start with the simplest GNU tools to validate our libc:

$FG,9$Trivial (no dependencies):$FG$
  1. true   - Just returns 0
  2. false  - Just returns 1
  3. yes    - Print "y" forever
  4. echo   - Print arguments

$FG,9$Simple I/O:$FG$
  5. cat    - Copy stdin to stdout
  6. head   - First N lines
  7. tail   - Last N lines
  8. wc     - Word/line/char count
  9. tee    - Copy stdin to stdout and file

$FG,9$Simple string:$FG$
  10. basename - Strip directory
  11. dirname  - Extract directory
  12. tr       - Character translation
  13. cut      - Extract fields

$FG,9$Test procedure:$FG$
  1. Get source from GNU coreutils
  2. Identify #includes needed
  3. Add stubs for missing functions
  4. Cross-compile or translate
  5. Test on ZealOS
  6. Fix issues, iterate

$FG,2$========================================================================
PHASE 8: Expand to Coreutils Subset
========================================================================$FG$

$FG,5$Priority: GOAL$FG$

Once simple tools work, expand to more complex ones:

$FG,9$File operations:$FG$
  ls, cp, mv, rm, mkdir, rmdir, touch, chmod, chown, ln

$FG,9$Text processing:$FG$
  sort, uniq, grep, sed, awk

$FG,9$System info:$FG$
  uname, date, hostname, whoami, id, env, printenv

$FG,9$Each tool may require additional libc functions.$FG$
Track missing functions and implement as needed.

$FG,2$========================================================================
IMPLEMENTATION ORDER (RECOMMENDED)
========================================================================$FG$

$FG,9$Week 1-2: Foundation$FG$
  [x] Phase 1: Basic headers and type definitions
  [x] Phase 3: _start, argc/argv parsing
  [ ] Phase 5: String functions (most are trivial)

$FG,9$Week 3-4: I/O$FG$
  [ ] Phase 4: FILE* and basic stdio
  [ ] Phase 4: printf (complex but critical)
  [ ] Test: echo, cat, true, false

$FG,9$Week 5-6: Memory and Process$FG$
  [ ] Phase 5: malloc/free (use ZealOS MAlloc/Free)
  [ ] Phase 6: system(), exec family
  [ ] Test: More coreutils

$FG,9$Week 7+: Expansion$FG$
  [ ] Phase 7-8: Port more tools
  [ ] Add missing functions as discovered
  [ ] Optimize and stabilize

$FG,2$========================================================================
FILES ALREADY AVAILABLE
========================================================================$FG$

From our POSIX layer:
  - File descriptors: open, close, read, write, lseek
  - Directories: opendir, readdir, closedir
  - Process: fork (emulated), exec, wait, waitpid
  - Environment: getenv, setenv, unsetenv
  - Pipes: pipe, pipe2
  - Signals: signal, kill
  - Time: time, gettimeofday, sleep, usleep

From ZealOS:
  - Memory: MAlloc, Free, MemCpy, MemSet, MemCmp
  - Strings: StrLen, StrCpy, StrCmp, StrCat, StrNew
  - File I/O: FOpen, FClose, FRead, FWrite, FileRead, FileWrite
  - Print: Print, PrintF, StrPrintF

$FG,2$========================================================================
QUICK START: Minimal libc for "Hello World"
========================================================================$FG$

To compile and run a simple C program:

$FG,9$hello.c:$FG$
  #include <stdio.h>
  int main() {
      printf("Hello, ZealOS!\n");
      return 0;
  }

$FG,9$Minimum requirements:$FG$
  1. _start calls main(argc, argv)
  2. printf writes to stdout
  3. exit(0) terminates cleanly

$FG,9$Implementation in HolyC:$FG$
  // Wrap the C program
  I64 main(I64 argc, U8 **argv);  // Forward declare

  U0 _start() {
      I64 ret = main(__argc, __argv);
      exit(ret);
  }

  // Map printf to ZealOS Print
  I64 printf(U8 *fmt, ...) {
      // Use va_args and Print
  }

$FG,5$This gives us a starting point to iterate from!$FG$
